% ============================================================================
% band-emitter — for three of the seven electromagnetic-spectrum bands, WHO
% or WHAT the SAME already-cited NASA sentence states emits that band
% (adj-facts-stdlib, PHYSICS).
% ============================================================================
% A sibling to the already-shipped `em-spectrum.adj`
% (`band_use(band, application)`, a representative everyday USE / effect /
% detector NASA associates with each of the seven EM bands — radio →
% radio_stations, x_ray → teeth, etc.). That table's own header already
% quotes, verbatim, three NASA sentences that separately state WHO or
% WHAT emits the band — a fact distinct from the `application` column
% (use/effect/detector), which the parent schema had no room for:
%
%   ultraviolet → sun
%     "Ultraviolet radiation is emitted by the Sun and are the reason
%      skin tans and burns."
%
%   infrared → skin_and_heat_objects
%     "Night vision goggles pick up the infrared light emitted by our
%      skin and objects with heat."
%
%   visible → fireflies
%     "Our eyes detect visible light. Fireflies, light bulbs, and stars
%      all emit visible light." (the source lists THREE emitters for
%      visible light; the FIRST listed, fireflies, is carried as the row
%      value, matching this stdlib's own established convention — see
%      `simple-machines.adj` — of carrying the first everyday item a
%      source lists when more than one is given.)
%
% Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? band_emitter(ultraviolet, $Emitter)   % sun
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5, a TWO-column table): each
% `row` lowers to a ground relation `band_emitter(band, emitter)` carrying
% the citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query —
% the engine returns the emitter WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero
% model calls, and abstains on anything that is not one of these three
% bands. `radio`, `microwave`, `x_ray`, and `gamma_ray` are deliberately
% NOT rows here: their own already-quoted NASA sentences state a USE
% (already captured by `em-spectrum.adj`'s own `application` column) but
% never an emitter, so this sibling is HONESTLY NARROWER than its
% parent — it grounds exactly the three bands whose cited span states an
% emitter, and abstains on the other four, or any other word, rather than
% inventing one. The query also runs BACKWARD — bind an emitter and
% recall which band it emits:
%
%     ? band_emitter($Band, sun)   % ultraviolet
%
% Provenance: the NASA Goddard Space Flight Center "Imagine the Universe!"
% page `em-spectrum.adj` cites. This paragraph used to say its spans were
% reproduced "byte-for-byte" from that table's header with "no new
% WebFetch", and that an ADJ `table` carries ONE provenance envelope; that
% header quotes "Our eyes detect visible light." and only notes that the
% page continues with the fireflies sentence, and row blocks now let each
% row carry its own span (see below). `trust authoritative` — the same
% tier `em-spectrum.adj`
% already carries (NASA Goddard Space Flight Center, a primary U.S.
% government teaching resource).
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
%
% EACH ROW CARRIES ITS OWN SENTENCE (ADJ-TABLES RS-5e, #14986). The envelope
% used to be the ultraviolet sentence, the primary source of all three
% answers, with the infrared and visible spans as table-level `cites`. Now
% the ultraviolet and infrared rows each take the sentence quoted for them
% above. The visible row takes only "Fireflies, light bulbs, and stars all
% emit visible light.", which names the band and its emitter; "Our eyes
% detect visible light." names a detector, not an emitter. The envelope is
% now a framing sentence from the page, which names no band and no emitter:
%
%     "The electromagnetic (EM) spectrum is the range of all types of EM
%      radiation."
%
% Measured 2026-09-15 on the raw page (HTTP 200; a nonsense path on the same
% host returns 404), with inline tags removed without a space and only ASCII
% whitespace collapsed: the three row sentences and the envelope each occur
% exactly once, each inside a `<p>`. Every row's page is the envelope's, so
% no row restates a `locator` line or `trust` (ADJ-TABLES §4).
% ============================================================================

table band_emitter {
    columns band, emitter

    row (ultraviolet, sun) {
        source "Ultraviolet radiation is emitted by the Sun and are the reason skin tans and burns."
    }
    row (infrared, skin_and_heat_objects) {
        source "Night vision goggles pick up the infrared light emitted by our skin and objects with heat."
    }
    row (visible, fireflies) {
        source "Fireflies, light bulbs, and stars all emit visible light."
    }

    % THE ENVELOPE WAS THE ULTRAVIOLET ROW'S OWN SENTENCE, the primary source
    % of all three answers, and the infrared and visible emitters were read
    % across two table-level `cites`. Each row now carries its own sentence,
    % and the envelope is a framing sentence that names no band and no
    % emitter. Every row overrides it, so it reaches no answer.
    source "The electromagnetic (EM) spectrum is the range of all types of EM radiation."
    locator "https://imagine.gsfc.nasa.gov/science/toolbox/emspectrum1.html"
    trust authoritative
}
